  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nA cold front will move across the region later this morning/ early\nafternoon, bringing increasing west winds gusting in the 20s and\n30s mph across the area. A period of locally higher gusts over the\nhigher foothills west of Billings and the Beartooth Mountains in\nthe 40s to 50 mph in the afternoon. The chance of precipitation \nwill increase with scattered showers and weak thunderstorms. Due \nto the frontal timing being earlier in the day, the chance for \nstrong to severe thunderstorms is very low at this time. \nInstability looks to be quite marginal with models depicting only \n100-500 J/kg of SBCAPE across the much of the forecast area. Out \nin the east along the Dakotas border, over up to 2000 J/kg is \ndepicted ahead of the front which is the big caveat. The best \ninstability exists well before peak daytime heating and the best \nforcing mechanism for tomorrow arrives too soon for the threat of \nstronger storms. The primary threats with any showers and \nthunderstorms tomorrow will be brief downpours and erratic wind \ngusts up to 40 mph. Look for the majority of shower/storm activity\nto occur behind the front in the afternoon and evening. \n\nThe upper low driving this cold front will continue sitting over\nsouthern Alberta through Wednesday, bringing widespread west to\nnorthwest winds gusting in the 20s to 40 mph. Locally higher gusts\nin the foothills west of Billings are forecast to climb towards\nthe low 50s mph. The best chance for these higher gusts exists\nalong the US-191 corridor near Harlowton (around 75 percent) and \nBig Timber (around 50 percent). Another round of showers and weak\nthunderstorms is forecast along the Montana and Wyoming border.\nThe greatest chance will be over the mountains and lowest over the\nplain of south-eastern Montana. As the low moves east Wednesday\nnight and into Thursday morning, a moderate chance of \nprecipitation along with increasing northwest 850mb winds will \nspread across the area.\n\nLook for conditions to become drier by Thursday afternoon and into\nFriday. Another weather system looks to drop into the region \nSaturday into early Sunday, bringing an additional chance of \nprecipitation to the area (low to moderate chance).\n\nWith the precipitation Tuesday into the weekend, cooler \ntemperatures will favor snow showers over the higher elevations of\nthe mountains at times (generally above 8000 feet). With this, \nsome impacts to recreation and travel in the high country are \npossible. This includes travel over the highest elevations of the \nBeartooth Highway in the Beartooth Mountains and Highway 14 \nthrough the Bighorn Mountains.\n\nLook for high temperatures in the 70s most days into the weekend,\nwith slightly cooler temperatures in the mid to upper 60s and low\n70s on Wednesday. LA/PM",
